+Tunnel inner-flow descent
+=========================
+
+For UDP tunnels -- VXLAN, Geneve, GTP-U, and the FOU/GUE UDP encapsulations --
+the dissector stops at the outer UDP header by default. Every inner flow riding
+one tunnel between a pair of endpoints then collapses onto a single value of
+``skb->hash``, so hash-based consumers cannot spread or distinguish them. GTP-U
+is the sharpest case: it has no outer source-port entropy at all, and it is the
+mobile-core data path where many subscriber flows share one tunnel.
+
+The ``*_inner`` gates make the dissector descend into the inner flow and hash on
+the inner 5-tuple instead. Unlike the byte-identical shapes, **enabling a
+descent changes** ``skb->hash`` for that traffic (outer identity becomes inner
+identity), so the descents are off by default, gated separately, and are a
+deliberate operator choice rather than a transparent optimisation. Once a
+descent is enabled it is byte-identical between the fast and slow paths -- both
+descend the same way -- so the choice is only *whether* to descend, not which
+path ran.
+
 When to enable
 ==============
 
@@ -123,7 +142,8 @@ A separate RFC proposes an optional ``auto`` mode built on these counters: the
 kernel would sample them over a packet-count window and flip the
 byte-identical gates itself, with hysteresis and a flip-rate cap, so the
 operator does not have to tune each shape by hand. That controller is not part
-of this series.
+of this series. The tunnel-descent gates would **never** be auto-managed,
+because enabling one changes hashing -- that must remain a deliberate action.
 
